The four smallest >3 are 5,7,11,13 sum 36. To get 38 with four distinct odd primes all >3: We need increase total by 2 from {5,7,11,13}. Any increase by changing a prime to a larger prime increases by at least 2, but must avoid duplicates and primality. If replace 13 by next prime 17, sum 40 (not 38); replace 11 by 13 duplicate? Actually replacing 11 by 13 duplicates 13, sum 38? 5+7+13+13=38 but not distinct. Replace 7 by 9 not prime; 5 by 7 duplicate. More systematic: The only possible four-prime distinct set summing 38? Let's enumerate: all odd primes >=5. Since sum 38 and four numbers, average 9.5. Possibilities include 5,7,11,15 no; 5,7,13,13 no; 5,11,? no. Could prove by parity/mod? But easier: The only four distinct odd primes >3 with sum 38: Sort a<b<c<d. Minimum a,b,c,d =5,7,11,13 sum36. To reach 38, exactly one of them increased by 2 (or one by 4 and another decreased? Can't below min). Since they are distinct primes, options:
- increase d from 13 to 15 not prime;
- increase c from 11 to 13 duplicates d;
- increase b from 7 to 9 not prime;
- increase a from 5 to 7 duplicates b.
